WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.380
Idea three says every cell comes from a cell.

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.280
The first scientists to argue for cells everywhere did not think so.

3
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.540
So where did they think new cells came from?

4
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.040
By the 1830s, improved microscopes were in use.

5
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.260
The argument was not only about seeing.

6
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.380
In 1838, Matthias Schleiden described plant tissues as made of cells.

7
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:35.480
In 1839, Theodor Schwann compared plant and animal tissues, such as tadpole

8
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.040
cartilage. He found the same cellular plan.

9
00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.290
Comparison was the key step.

10
00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:47.030
Many kinds of tissue, plant and animal, showed one shared plan.

11
00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.930
That was evidence that plants and animals alike are built of cells.

12
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.630
But Schleiden and Schwann also thought new cells could form inside a structureless

13
00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:01.370
fluid. Picture a crystal growing in a solution.

14
00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.310
No cell divides to make it.

15
00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:07.090
Schleiden proposed this for plants.

16
00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:09.950
Schwann extended it to animals.

17
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.300
So there were two models.

18
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:18.600
In the fluid model, a new cell forms inside a structureless fluid, and no cell

19
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.780
divides. In the division model, one existing cell splits into two.

20
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.540
Suppose you could watch a growing tissue for a while.

21
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.080
What would each model lead you to expect to find?

22
00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:42.727
The fluid model predicts new cells taking shape out of fluid, with no cell dividing.

23
00:01:44.027 --> 00:01:46.507
The division model predicts a series of stages.

24
00:01:47.927 --> 00:01:51.007
One cell pinches in two, then its daughters split again.

25
00:01:52.407 --> 00:01:55.007
Watching over time can tell the two models apart.

26
00:01:56.067 --> 00:02:02.467
In 1852, Robert Remak published evidence that new cells form when existing cells

27
00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:09.247
divide. In 1855, Rudolf Virchow spread the idea widely in a short article.

28
00:02:10.587 --> 00:02:11.967
Every cell comes from a cell.

29
00:02:13.307 --> 00:02:16.607
Remak's evidence came first; Virchow made it widely known.

30
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.360
The fluid idea was wrong, so it was dropped.

31
00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.520
The core stayed: plants and animals are made of cells.

32
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:30.720
A corrected idea took the fluid idea's place: cells come from existing cells.

33
00:02:31.783 --> 00:02:34.003
That is how a scientific theory works.

34
00:02:35.203 --> 00:02:39.303
It is a well-tested explanation, tied to many lines of evidence.

35
00:02:40.603 --> 00:02:43.583
When strong new evidence appears, it can be revised.

36
00:02:44.963 --> 00:02:46.863
Being revised does not make it a guess.

37
00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:49.393
One limit on the rule.

38
00:02:50.673 --> 00:02:53.753
Every cell comes from a cell is about cells living today.

39
00:02:54.973 --> 00:02:59.773
How the very first cells arose is a separate question that scientists still study.
